-- The `terminal` module is the language's answer to "how do I say something
-- to a human?" — there is no core print statement, so a program emits a
-- particle to a linked module and that module writes it out. `Print` is the
-- plain case: it renders `value` and writes it, newline-terminated, straight
-- to stdout. Being a `.so`, it does this in C in both output modes (the
-- compiled binary dlopens the very same library the interpreter does), so the
-- bytes on the wire are identical either way.
--
-- The result is a particle carrying how many characters landed on the wire,
-- so a program can prove the print happened without the harness having to
-- capture and compare stdout.
link "native_modules/terminal.so" as term
emit Print { "value": "hello, terminal" } to term get r
assert r = { "_class": "TerminalResult", "value": 15 }
